<Item XName="Read_me" UserName="Read_me" Type="Notes">
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;Discovering the Phased Array &lt;BR&gt;Beamforming Kit&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies SystemVue Example&amp;nbsp;Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;o&quot; ns = &quot;urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;This interactive&amp;nbsp;tutorial example builds a simple RF Beamformer in 3 different ways, beginning with Spectrasys &quot;Phased Array&quot; analysis.&amp;nbsp; Each technique has its own distinct advantages.&amp;nbsp; Note that &quot;sliders&quot; have been provided in many of the schematics for interactive tuning.&amp;nbsp; Many (but not all) of these parameters are also variable during a dynamic simulation, in what is called &quot;run-time tuning.&quot;&amp;nbsp; Specifically, if you want to change the actual array size (&quot;NumEl&quot;) or TX vs. RX mode (&quot;ArrayState&quot;), you will need to stop the simulation, adjust the value, and then re-start the simulation for it to take effect.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff0000 size=2&gt;Since the Main1 design can be simulated in both Rx and Tx mode, some graphs (Sweep1_GOverTAtThetaPhi)&amp;nbsp;and tables (RxArrayMeasurements, TxxArrayMeasurements)&amp;nbsp;will produce errors depending on what mode is simulated, since they graph/show measurements available ONLY in one of the modes.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff0000 size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff0000 size=2&gt;The System1 analysis (in the folder &quot;1 Spectrasys TX Beamforming&quot;) defines two paths, one for Rx and one for Tx configuration, and they are both enabled. Since the system can ONLY&amp;nbsp;be in the Rx or Tx state for any single simulation, when you run the System1 analysis you will get&amp;nbsp;the warning &quot;No signal source spectrum could be found for path ...&quot; for the path that does not correspond to the current state (Rx or Tx) of the system. You can safely ignore this warning. If you want to remove this warning, you can open the System1 analysis and disable the path that does not correpsond to the current state (Rx or Tx) of the system.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff0000 size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Folders in this workspace:&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;1.&amp;nbsp;Spectrasys TX Beamforming&lt;/STRONG&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Do a basic &quot;Phased Array&quot; analysis that builds on top of Spectrasys.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Learn the basic simulation set-up for a TX vs. RX arrays.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Play with various parameters to see their effect, such as Windowing/Tapers, number of array elements, and more.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;2.&amp;nbsp;Spectrasys vs. Frequency&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Now build some additional complexity by sweeping the frequency, and looking at beam characteristics vs. freq (&quot;Squinting&quot;).&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;3. Dataflow TX beamforming&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Now let&apos;s&amp;nbsp;move to the system (&quot;Dataflow&quot;) layer in the time domain.&amp;nbsp; How is beamforming done in this simulator?&amp;nbsp; Setup, analysis.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The &quot;Timed Envelope Matrix&quot; datatype.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;4. Dataflow&lt;/STRONG&gt; &lt;STRONG&gt;beamforming,&amp;nbsp;using RFLINK &lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Leverage Spectrasys&amp;nbsp;model from Step 1 at the&amp;nbsp;Dataflow Simulation Level&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Note that it still allows dynamic RF gains &amp;amp; phases.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;</Text>
<Item XName="Page" Type="PageMgr">
